window.PYWEB_SEARCH=[{"u":"about.html","p":"About Python","h":"About Python","x":"Home / About Python How a website works Simple syntax, a powerful ecosystem and mature frameworks make Python a convenient language to start web development with. Just starting out? First go through the “From scratch” lessons , and look up unfamiliar words in the glossary ."},{"u":"about.html#sec-1","p":"About Python","h":"What backend development is","x":"The backend is the part of an application that runs on the server: it receives requests, handles the logic, talks to the database and sends a response. Handling requests The framework receives an HTTP request and decides which code should handle it. Database The application reads and saves users, products, articles and other data through an ORM or SQL. Building the response The server returns HTML, JSON or a file: what the user finally sees. Logic and security Checking data, access rights, handling errors and preparing a correct response."},{"u":"about.html#sec-2","p":"About Python","h":"What Python does on the server","x":"The server receives requests, works with data and returns responses. This is what Python code does for that. Matches the page address to the right function (routing). Checks the data the user sent, for example from a form. Reads and writes data in the database. Checks access rights and user logins. Builds HTML pages from templates. Gives data in JSON format to other programs."},{"u":"about.html#sec-3","p":"About Python","h":"How a user's request travels","x":"A simplified chain from the click to the server's response. 1 Browser The user opens a page or submits a form. 2 HTTP request The request goes to the server: method, address and data. 3 Framework Django or Flask finds the right route and handler. 4 Logic and DB The code checks the data and reads or saves information. 5 Response The server returns a page, JSON or an error."},{"u":"about.html#flow-title","p":"About Python","h":"The request's path step by step","x":"What happens in a split second after you press the “Add task” button. Browser HTML, CSS, JS Web server nginx, WSGI Application Flask, Django Database SQLite, PostgreSQL The browser sends a request. The form becomes a POST /tasks request with headers, cookies and the form data. The web server receives it. It terminates the HTTPS connection, serves static files itself and passes the rest to the application through WSGI. The framework finds the route. The address /tasks and the POST method point to your function, which receives the request object. The function checks the data. Is the user logged in, is there a CSRF token, is the title empty? On an error it returns a 400 or 403 response right away. The database saves the record. An INSERT INTO tasks ... with parameters runs, and the database returns the new row's number. The application prepares the response. This is a redirect to the list, HTML from a template or JSON for an API. The web server sends the response. The status code, headers and body travel back over the network. The browser shows the result. It follows the redirect, builds the page and loads the CSS, JavaScript and images."},{"u":"about.html#sec-5","p":"About Python","h":"Python's strengths on the web","x":"The main reasons the language stays a popular choice for server-side development. Why Python is good for starting web development Feature Why it matters Readable syntax Code is easier to maintain and review in a team, and beginners get in more easily. Ready-made frameworks Django and Flask cover most typical server-side tasks. Library ecosystem It is easy to add a database, authentication, API tools and analytics. Link to data analysis One language can be used for the web, automation and data analysis."},{"u":"about.html#sec-6","p":"About Python","h":"Code examples","x":"A minimal Flask server and basic work with SQLite: two important entry points. Flask: a minimal server SQLite: basic work with data Note: the examples are for learning, and no server runs in this project. Here you can only read and copy the code."},{"u":"about.html#sec-7","p":"About Python","h":"A mini glossary","x":"Terms that help you read web development material with more confidence. Backend The server side of an application, which handles requests, stores data and returns responses. Frontend The client side of a website: the interface, buttons, forms and everything the user sees in the browser. HTTP The protocol the browser and server use to exchange requests and responses. ORM A tool that lets you work with a database through objects and code instead of hand-written SQL queries. API An interface through which one program can get data or functions from another program. SQLite A lightweight database often used for learning and small projects."},{"u":"about.html#sec-8","p":"About Python","h":"Practical checklist","x":"What you can do after studying this section. Explain how a server differs from a browser. Understand why an application needs a database. Start a simple Flask server. Create one route and return some text. Make a table in SQLite and add a record to it. Compare which tasks are better solved with a framework than by hand."},{"u":"about.html#check-title","p":"About Python","h":"Test yourself","x":"Four questions on this section. An explanation appears after you answer. In the user's browser On the server In the database The backend runs on the server: it receives the request, runs the logic and returns a response. The frontend runs in the browser: HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Makes pages load faster Saves data to the database Matches the page address to the function that handles it A route links an address (for example, / or /about ) to a Python function. In Flask it is the @app.route decorator, in Django the urlpatterns list. JSON A PNG image A Word document JSON is a “key: value” text format that both JavaScript and mobile apps read easily. This is how APIs work. The server queries the database The browser sends an HTTP request to the server The framework builds the response The chain starts in the browser: request → the framework finds the route → logic and database → response. See the “How a user's request travels” diagram above."},{"u":"about.html#sources-title","p":"About Python","h":"Sources","x":"Checked on 06.11.2026 . The examples are for Python 3.12 and newer. MDN: an overview of HTTP — request, response, methods Python: the sqlite3 module — working with SQLite from the standard library Flask 3.1: Quickstart — a minimal app and routes Python HOWTO — guides on individual topics of the language"},{"u":"about.html#sec-11","p":"About Python","h":"Where to go next?","x":"Once the basics are clear, the next step is to choose a framework: Django or Flask. Compare Django and Flask"},{"u":"about.html#sec-12","p":"About Python","h":"How it all connects","x":"You open a page, the browser sends a request to the server, a Python program finds the data and sends back a response. Below is the same as a list. Python The server side of a website (backend) Handling requests Working with a database Building the response Which address opens which page Django Admin panel and data handling A ready project structure A quick start for sites with records Flask Only the essentials Everything else is added as needed Good for small services"},{"u":"api.html","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","x":"Home / REST API REST API: a site that returns data You need an API when the server answers not with a page but with data: for a JavaScript site, a mobile app or another program."},{"u":"api.html#rest-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"The main rules of REST","x":"The address names the data, and the HTTP method names the action on it. HTTP methods for a task list Request What it does Successful response GET /tasks Returns the list 200 OK POST /tasks Creates a task 201 Created GET /tasks/5 Returns one task 200 OK PATCH /tasks/5 Changes some fields 200 OK DELETE /tasks/5 Deletes 204 No Content"},{"u":"api.html#status-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"Status codes","x":"The client looks at the code first and only then reads the response body. All good or a client error 200 — success 201 — created 204 — success, no response body 400 — invalid data 401 — you need to log in 403 — no permission 404 — not found Server error 500 — an error in the application code 502 — the web server got no proper response from the application 503 — the service is temporarily unavailable A 5xx error means the cause must be looked for on the server, not in the request."},{"u":"api.html#flask-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"An API in Flask","x":"Flask turns a dictionary into JSON itself. A list is better passed through jsonify . Flask: a JSON API with a task list"},{"u":"api.html#django-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"An API in Django","x":"A simple JSON response can be returned with built-in tools. For a large API people usually take Django REST framework . tasks/views.py The safe=False parameter is needed because by default JsonResponse accepts only a dictionary."},{"u":"api.html#client-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"How to call an API from the browser","x":"On a page this is done by the fetch function. Check the status code: fetch does not treat 404 as an error. JavaScript If the site and the API live at different addresses, the browser blocks the response until the server allows it with CORS headers. Flask has the Flask-CORS extension for this."},{"u":"api.html#fastapi-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"Where FastAPI fits","x":"If the project is only an API, have a look at FastAPI : it checks data against type hints and builds documentation itself. The REST principles stay the same."},{"u":"api.html#sources-title","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . MDN: HTTP methods MDN: HTTP status codes Flask: JSON and JavaScript Django 6.1: JsonResponse"},{"u":"api.html#sec-8","p":"REST API with Flask and Django for beginners","h":"Next: the second project","x":"Build a blog API with a real database. Open the section"},{"u":"auth.html","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","x":"Home / Authentication How a website recognises a user Authentication answers the question “who is this?”, authorisation answers “what are they allowed to do?”. We look at storing passwords, sessions and protected pages."},{"u":"auth.html#passwords-title","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Passwords are stored only as a hash","x":"A hash is an irreversible fingerprint. If the database is stolen, it contains no real passwords. Hashing a password Hashes of the same password differ because of a random “salt”, so identical passwords do not look the same in the database. A plain md5 or sha256 without salt and slowing down is not suitable for passwords. In a real application you can leave out the method: the library picks a strong default."},{"u":"auth.html#session-title","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Session: how a site remembers your login","x":"HTTP does not remember earlier requests. After you log in, the server gives the browser a cookie, and the browser attaches it to the next requests. Flask: login, a protected page and logout What to watch out for A Flask session is signed with secret_key but not encrypted: do not keep secrets in it. A wrong username and a wrong password get the same response, so nobody can find out which usernames exist. Logout is done with POST, so it cannot be triggered by a simple link on another site. On a real site, login must work only over HTTPS, and it is worth limiting the number of attempts."},{"u":"auth.html#django-title","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Authentication in Django","x":"Django already has users, password hashing, sessions and ready login pages. mysite/urls.py tasks/views.py mysite/settings.py You create the login template yourself: templates/registration/login.html . In current Django versions logout works only with POST, so make it a form with a button, not a link."},{"u":"auth.html#roles-title","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Access rights","x":"After login it is important to check that the user works only with their own data. A common mistake is to check only the login itself. If the address /tasks/5/delete is open to anyone logged in, someone else's task can be deleted just by changing the number. Always check the owner on the server: Django: only your own tasks"},{"u":"auth.html#sources-title","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . Flask: sessions Werkzeug: password hashing Django 6.1: the user system OWASP: password storage"},{"u":"auth.html#sec-6","p":"Authentication in Flask and Django: passwords and sessions","h":"Next: REST API","x":"A server can return not only pages but data too. Open the section"},{"u":"cheatsheet.html","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","x":"Home / Cheat sheet Cheat sheet The most common commands on one page. Activation commands are given for Windows; there is a separate line for macOS and Linux."},{"u":"cheatsheet.html#env-title","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"Environment and packages","x":"Create a separate environment for every project. Terminal"},{"u":"cheatsheet.html#flask-title","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"Flask","x":"A minimal app and the most useful functions. Terminal app.py"},{"u":"cheatsheet.html#django-title","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"Django","x":"manage.py commands and ORM basics. Terminal ORM Django"},{"u":"cheatsheet.html#sql-title","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"SQL","x":"The main queries. Pass values from the user only as parameters. SQL"},{"u":"cheatsheet.html#http-title","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"HTTP status codes","x":"More in the REST API section. Common status codes Code Meaning Code Meaning 200 Success 401 You need to log in 201 Created 403 No permission 204 Success, no body 404 Not found 301/302 Redirect 405 Method not allowed 400 Bad request 500 Server error"},{"u":"cheatsheet.html#git-title","p":"Cheat sheet: Python, Flask, Django, SQL, Git","h":"Git","x":"More in the Git section. Terminal"},{"u":"contacts.html","p":"Contact and author","h":"Contact and author","x":"Home / Contact Contact Ask the author a question or suggest an improvement. Through the form, your message goes to GitHub and is visible to everyone. If you have no GitHub account, write directly ."},{"u":"contacts.html#sec-1","p":"Contact and author","h":"Feedback form","x":"You need a free GitHub account: the form opens a new entry in Issues, and you only have to press the create button. Name Message A copy of the message is saved in this browser. More in the privacy policy . No GitHub account? Write to pywebguide@gmail.com by email. Only the author will see your message."},{"u":"contacts.html#sec-2","p":"Contact and author","h":"Information","x":"Brief facts about the project and the technology. Project PyWeb Guide, a web resource about Python in web development Author E. Zhanguzhin, group ISU(SOP)-25 Sections Python from scratch, About Python, Frameworks, Databases, First project, Deployment, Templates and forms, Authentication, REST API, Testing, Git, Second project; sandbox, exercises, cheat sheet, glossary and final test Technology HTML, CSS, JavaScript, responsive layout, animations Topics Python, backend, Django, Flask, databases, practice Format A learning project without its own server: messages are received by GitHub Issues, and without a GitHub account you can write by email or on Telegram"},{"u":"contacts.html#sec-3","p":"Contact and author","h":"Saved in this browser","x":"The latest messages you saved on this device."},{"u":"contacts.html#authors-title","p":"Contact and author","h":"About the author","x":"The resource began as a learning project for the course “Introduction to Web Design and Development” at Manash Kozybayev North Kazakhstan University (Petropavl). E. Zhanguzhin Student of group ISU(SOP)-25, author and developer of the project. Updates Last updated: 06.11.2026 . The code is open: repository on GitHub ."},{"u":"course.html","p":"Full course","h":"Full course","x":"Home / Full course Full course All sections in the recommended order. Progress is kept only in this browser, but you can move it to another device with a file."},{"u":"course.html#dash-title","p":"Full course","h":"My progress","x":"A summary of lessons, projects, exercises and the test, moving it with a file, and a certificate. Open my progress"},{"u":"course.html#map-title","p":"Full course","h":"Curriculum","x":"From the first steps to your own published service. Python from scratch 13 lessons right in the browser, nothing to install HTML and CSS Tags, links, forms and page styling About Python What the backend is and how a request travels Frameworks Django, Flask and FastAPI: which to choose Databases SQL, SQLite, ORM and migrations Setting up your computer Python, VS Code and the terminal First project A to-do list with Flask or Django Deployment Your site online on PythonAnywhere Templates and forms Jinja, data validation, CSRF Authentication Passwords, sessions and access rights REST API JSON, methods and status codes Testing pytest and the test client Git Commits, branches and GitHub Second project A blog API with a database and tests Final test 15 questions and a learning certificate"},{"u":"course.html#tools-title","p":"Full course","h":"Tools","x":"Useful at any stage. Sandbox Write and run Python right in the browser. Exercises Sixteen exercises with hints and instant automatic checking. Cheat sheet Common commands on one page, handy to print. Contact Ask the author a question."},{"u":"databases.html","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","x":"Home / Databases How a website stores data Users, tasks, products, comments: everything that must survive closing the tab is kept in a database. We will look at SQL using SQLite and show how the Django ORM does the same. Just starting out? First go through the “From scratch” lessons , and look up unfamiliar words in the glossary ."},{"u":"databases.html#why-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Why a server needs a database","x":"Variables in Python live only while the program runs. A database keeps data on disk and can find it quickly. Tables Data lives in tables: a row is one record (a product), a column is one property (name, price). Primary key Every row has a unique number, id . You can use it to find, change or delete the record. SQL The database query language: SELECT reads, INSERT adds, UPDATE changes, DELETE removes. ORM A layer that turns Python classes into tables and method calls into SQL queries."},{"u":"databases.html#sql-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"SQL in practice: the sqlite3 module","x":"SQLite is a database in a single file. It is built into Python and needs no server, so it is the best option for learning projects. Save the code to a file and run it: it creates shop.db . shop.py Do not forget commit() . Changes ( INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE ) reach the file only after conn.commit() . Without it the data is lost when the connection closes."},{"u":"databases.html#inject-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"The main rule: parameters instead of f-strings","x":"If you put the user's text straight into SQL, the user can change the query itself. This is called SQL injection, one of the most common vulnerabilities of websites. Dangerous: an f-string Safe: the ? parameter"},{"u":"databases.html#orm-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"The same with the Django ORM","x":"In Django a table is described by a model class, and queries are written as Python methods. Django builds the SQL itself and always passes values as parameters, so injection is not a worry here. shop/models.py python manage.py shell The same operations in SQL and in the Django ORM Action SQL ORM Django Add INSERT INTO products (name, price) VALUES (?, ?) Product.objects.create(name=…, price=…) Find by condition SELECT … FROM products WHERE price < 300 Product.objects.filter(price__lt=300) Change UPDATE products SET price = 400 WHERE … Product.objects.filter(…).update(price=400) Delete DELETE FROM products WHERE … Product.objects.filter(…).delete()"},{"u":"databases.html#migr-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Migrations: how a model becomes a table","x":"A migration is a file with instructions for how to change the database. Django creates it from changes in the models and then applies it. This way the database structure always matches the code and is easy to recreate on another computer. Terminal The SQL Django generated Note that PositiveIntegerField became the check CHECK (\"price\" >= 0) : the database itself will not save a negative price. The output was produced by the sqlmigrate command on Django 6.1 and SQLite."},{"u":"databases.html#choose-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Which database to choose","x":"Code with an ORM barely changes when you switch databases: only the connection settings change. Popular databases for Python projects Database When to use Features SQLite Learning projects, prototypes, small sites One file, built into Python, the default database in Django PostgreSQL Real projects with users A separate server, reliable under concurrent requests MySQL / MariaDB When your hosting provides it A separate server, common on hosting Flask has no ORM of its own. You can write SQL through sqlite3 , as in the second step of the Flask project , or add the Flask-SQLAlchemy extension."},{"u":"databases.html#check-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Test yourself","x":"Four questions on this section. An explanation appears after you answer. INSERT UPDATE SELECT UPDATE changes existing rows, INSERT adds new ones, SELECT only reads. With the ? parameter and a tuple of values With an f-string By adding strings together Parameters are passed separately from the query text, so user input cannot change the SQL. An f-string or string concatenation opens the door to SQL injection. Nothing, Django updates the table itself Delete the database file Run makemigrations, then migrate makemigrations creates a migration file from the model changes, migrate applies it to the database. There is no need to delete the database: the data stays. SQLite cannot store data You forgot to call conn.commit() You need to restart the computer Changing queries run in a transaction and are written to the file only after commit()."},{"u":"databases.html#sources-title","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Sources","x":"Examples checked on Python 3.13, SQLite and Django 6.1.3, 06.11.2026 . Python: the sqlite3 module — connecting, parameters, transactions Django 6.1: models — fields and relations Django 6.1: queries with the ORM — filter, update, delete Django 6.1: migrations OWASP: SQL injection — a description of the vulnerability"},{"u":"databases.html#sec-9","p":"Databases: SQL, SQLite and the Django ORM","h":"Try it in practice","x":"In the first project you will connect SQLite to Flask and work with a model in Django. Open the first project"},{"u":"deploy.html","p":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","h":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","x":"Home / Deployment How to put a website online While the server runs on your computer, only you can see the site. Let us see how to publish the to-do list from the first project for free on PythonAnywhere, a hosting service made for Python that needs no server setup."},{"u":"deploy.html#plan-title","p":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","h":"What you get and the limitations","x":"After publishing, the app opens at https://ваш-логин.pythonanywhere.com . The free Beginner plan One web app at логин.pythonanywhere.com 512 MB of disk: plenty for a learning project with SQLite The app runs for a month; then you extend it with a button on the Web tab Internet access from your code only to sites on an allowlist 100 CPU seconds a day for code in consoles; the site itself does not use them The order of steps Put the code on GitHub Copy it to PythonAnywhere and install the dependencies Create a web app and point it to the WSGI file For Django: set up the address and static files Press Reload and open the site The plan terms come from the PythonAnywhere help as of November 2026 and may change; current details are in the sources at the bottom of the page."},{"u":"deploy.html#deploy-steps-title","p":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","h":"Step-by-step guide","x":"In the commands, replace username with your PythonAnywhere username and USER/REPO with the address of your GitHub repository. Step 1. Prepare the project and put it on GitHub The requirements.txt file lists the project's libraries, and the server installs the same ones from it. The .gitignore file keeps the environment and the local database out of the repository. Create the repository on github.com first. Terminal (in the project folder) .gitignore Step 2. Copy the code to PythonAnywhere Sign up at pythonanywhere.com (Beginner plan), open the Consoles tab and start Bash . The mkvirtualenv command creates an environment and activates it right away. Terminal (Bash console on PythonAnywhere) Python 3.13 is available only in the innit system image. If the command says python3.13 is not found, open the Account page, the System image tab, and choose innit. Step 3. Create a web app On the Web tab, press Add a new web app , choose Manual configuration (not the ready Flask or Django template, which creates an empty project) and the same Python 3.13. Then type mysite-venv in the Virtualenv section: the path completes itself. Step 4. Set up the WSGI file The WSGI file tells the server where "},{"u":"deploy.html#update-title","p":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","h":"How to update the site and what not to do","x":"Terminal: updating after code changes Security Do not leave DEBUG = True on the server: on an error, visitors would see your settings and pieces of code. The SECRET_KEY from settings.py must not be in a public repository: keep it in an environment variable. Do not push a database with real data to GitHub: that is why .gitignore contains *.db ."},{"u":"deploy.html#sources-title","p":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","h":"Sources","x":"PythonAnywhere help checked on 06.11.2026 . PythonAnywhere: deploying Flask PythonAnywhere: deploying an existing Django project PythonAnywhere: free account features PythonAnywhere: Python versions in system images PythonAnywhere: environment variables — where to keep SECRET_KEY Django 6.1: deployment checklist"},{"u":"deploy.html#sec-5","p":"Deploying Flask and Django to PythonAnywhere","h":"No project to publish yet?","x":"Build a to-do list with Flask or Django: it takes from half an hour. Open the first project"},{"u":"exam.html","p":"Final test on Python in web development","h":"Final test on Python in web development","x":"Home / Final test Final test 15 questions on the whole course. Answer 12 or more correctly, and you can download a learning certificate. The number of attempts is unlimited."},{"u":"exam.html#test-title","p":"Final test on Python in web development","h":"Questions","x":"After checking, an explanation appears under each question. In the user's browser On the server In the database The backend runs on the server: it receives the request, runs the logic and returns a response. Makes pages load faster Saves data to the database Links an address to the function that handles it In Flask it is the @app.route decorator, in Django the urlpatterns list. To keep the project's libraries separate from the rest of the system To make the code run faster To protect the site from hacking Every project can have its own library versions, and they will not get in each other's way. Flask Django Jinja Django comes with an ORM and an admin. Flask is minimal, and Jinja is a template language. Moving a site to another host Updating Django itself A file describing a change to the database structure The makemigrations command creates such a file, and migrate applies it to the database. As a parameter: execute(\"... WHERE id = ?\", (user_id,)) With an f-string: execute(f\"... WHERE id = {user_id}\") Join the strings with a plus Parameters protect against SQL injection. Putting the value into the string lets the query itself be changed. Outputs the value as ready HTML Outputs the"},{"u":"frameworks.html","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","x":"Home / Frameworks Django or Flask: which to choose Both frameworks solve the same task, building the server side of a website, but in different ways: with a ready-made architecture or with a flexible minimal core. At the end of the page there is a short note on a third popular option, FastAPI. Just starting out? First go through the “From scratch” lessons , and look up unfamiliar words in the glossary . The comparison is current for Django 6.1 and Flask 3.1 (November 2026)."},{"u":"frameworks.html#two-title","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Two approaches","x":"The main difference is how many ready-made solutions the framework gives you straight away. Django “Batteries included”: a framework with a full set of tools. A built-in ORM for working with the database A ready admin panel for managing content A strict project structure and a single code style Well suited to medium and large applications Fewer hand-made solutions for typical tasks Flask A microframework: a minimal core and maximum flexibility. Very little imposed structure Extensions are added as needed Easier to learn from scratch and to understand how the server works Good for small services, learning projects and APIs More control over the architecture"},{"u":"frameworks.html#sec-2","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Comparison by criteria","x":"Seven things people most often choose a framework by. Django and Flask: comparison by criteria Criterion Django Flask Framework level Full-featured (“batteries included”) A microframework with a minimal core Getting started You need to learn the project structure, but much is ready The first app takes a few lines Database Built-in ORM and migrations Added through extensions and libraries Admin panel Included out of the box No, added separately Flexibility Medium: there is a “right way” High: you choose the structure yourself Best suited to Large sites with users and data Small services, prototypes and APIs Example use An online shop, a news portal A notes service, a learning API What a beginner should choose A short tip for your first project. If you want to understand quickly how a server works, start with Flask: it has little “magic”. If you need a site with users and an admin panel, take Django. Both options are good. The main thing is to finish your first learning project. Typical mistakes when choosing What is best avoided. Choosing by fashion rather than by the task. Taking Flask for a big project and hand-building what Django already has. Taking Django for a tiny page. Switc"},{"u":"frameworks.html#sec-3","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"When to use what","x":"A short practical table for choosing a framework for a task. Recommendations for choosing between Django and Flask Task Recommendation A learning project and a first look at the backend Flask: it is easier to see how everything works inside. A site with an admin panel and many data models Django: the admin panel and ORM save time. A small REST API Flask: very little extra code. A project that will grow and be maintained by a team Django: a single structure reduces chaos in the code."},{"u":"frameworks.html#sec-4","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"The same page in two frameworks","x":"The code shows the difference in boilerplate for the simplest route. Flask Django: views.py"},{"u":"frameworks.html#sec-5","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Mini quiz: what suits you better?","x":"Answer five questions and get a framework recommendation. There are no right answers here: the quiz only points you in a direction. Choose one option in each question Understanding how a server works inside Getting a finished result faster No, I will assemble the parts I need myself Yes, it saves a lot of time A site with users, content and a database A small service, an API or a learning example I want ready rules and order I want to decide myself how everything is arranged I will pick the libraries I need myself I want built-in database tools What to do next: Build your first project Official documentation"},{"u":"frameworks.html#fastapi-title","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"What about FastAPI?","x":"FastAPI is the third popular Python framework. It is made mainly for APIs: the server returns data as JSON, and the interface is built separately (a JavaScript site or a mobile app). How it differs Input data is checked using Python type annotations (the Pydantic library) Automatic interactive API documentation at /docs Support for asynchronous code ( async def ) out of the box No built-in ORM or admin panel: as in Flask, they are added separately FastAPI: main.py Django, Flask and FastAPI: what to choose for the task Task Django Flask FastAPI A site with HTML pages and an admin panel Best choice Suitable Not its main purpose A JSON API for a frontend or a mobile app Suitable (with Django REST framework) Suitable Best choice A first look at the backend Suitable Best choice Suitable if you know type annotations"},{"u":"frameworks.html#sources-title","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Sources","x":"Versions checked on PyPI on 06.11.2026 . Django 6.1 documentation — current version 6.1.3, requires Python 3.12+ Flask 3.1 documentation — current version 3.1.3 FastAPI documentation — current version 0.162.0 Django REST framework — building APIs with Django Django release support dates — which versions still get fixes"},{"u":"frameworks.html#sec-8","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Frequently asked questions","x":"Short answers to typical beginner questions. Which is easier to learn first? If you want to understand quickly how a server works, start with Flask. If you need a project with a ready admin panel and a database, Django is a convenient start. Do I need to know SQL? It helps. Even with an ORM, a basic understanding of SQL makes it easier to work with data and debug queries. Can I move from Flask to Django later? Yes. Python stays the same, so the move is possible, but you will have to learn a different project architecture and Django's built-in tools."},{"u":"frameworks.html#sec-9","p":"Django, Flask or FastAPI","h":"Still have questions?","x":"Write through the feedback form: you can ask about choosing a framework, databases or your first project. Go to contacts"},{"u":"git.html","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","x":"Home / Git Git: the history of your project Git remembers versions of your code, lets you go back to a working state and work together. GitHub is a website where copies of such projects are kept."},{"u":"git.html#start-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"The first commit","x":"A commit is a saved snapshot of the project with a clear description. Terminal: once per computer Terminal: in the project folder The three states of a file Modified: you changed the file, but Git has not saved it yet. Staged: the git add command added it to the next commit. Committed: the git commit command wrote the snapshot into the history."},{"u":"git.html#ignore-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"What not to keep in Git","x":"The .gitignore file lists what Git should not track. .gitignore If a password or key has already reached a commit on GitHub, deleting the file is not enough: it stays in the history. Treat the key as exposed and replace it with a new one."},{"u":"git.html#branches-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"Branches","x":"A branch lets you build a new feature separately without breaking the working version. Terminal If there is a conflict A conflict means two branches changed the same line. Open the file, keep the version you need, remove the markers <<<<<<< , ======= and >>>>>>> , then run git add and git commit ."},{"u":"git.html#github-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"Working with GitHub","x":"First create an empty repository on github.com. Terminal: the first push Terminal: an ordinary day"},{"u":"git.html#undo-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"How to undo changes","x":"Before undoing anything, look at the current state with git status . What to do in different situations Situation Command Important Unstage a file git restore --staged app.py The edits in the file stay Discard edits in a file git restore app.py Unsaved edits are lost for good Undo a commit that is already pushed git revert abc1234 Creates a new commit, the history is not broken Fix the message of the last commit git commit --amend Only if the commit has not been pushed yet The commands git reset --hard and git push --force can delete work for good. A beginner is better off without them."},{"u":"git.html#messages-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"A good commit message","x":"Describe what changed and why. In a month you will read the history like the project's diary. Clear Add a check for an empty task Fix login with a wrong password Unclear edits fix again"},{"u":"git.html#sources-title","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . The Pro Git book (in Russian; also available in English) GitHub: getting started GitHub: removing sensitive data from a repository"},{"u":"git.html#sec-8","p":"Git and GitHub for beginner web developers","h":"Next: deployment","x":"Your code is on GitHub. All that is left is to put the site online. Open the section"},{"u":"glossary.html","p":"Web development glossary in plain words","h":"Web development glossary in plain words","x":"Home / Glossary Glossary in plain words What the words on this site mean. If you forget one of them, look here. Find a word"},{"u":"glossary.html#sec-1","p":"Web development glossary in plain words","h":"How a website works","x":"Browser The program you open websites in: Chrome, Safari, Firefox. Server A computer that is always on and connected to the internet. The website's program runs on it. Request, response and HTTP The browser asks the server for a page: that is a request. The server sends it back: that is a response. HTTP is the set of rules they use to talk. Frontend Everything you see on the page: text, buttons, design. It runs in the browser. Backend The hidden part of a website: a program on the server that receives the request, finds the data and prepares the response. This is where Python works. Route A rule for which address opens which page. For example, the address /contacts opens the contact page. HTML The content of a page: headings, paragraphs, links, images. CSS The look of a page: colours, fonts, layout of blocks. JavaScript A programming language that runs in the browser. It brings the page to life: buttons, menus, form checks. 127.0.0.1, localhost and port The address 127.0.0.1 (also called localhost) means “this same computer”. A port is the number of the “door” a program is waiting behind. The address http://127.0.0.1:5000 opens the site running on your machine through door 5000. Ot"},{"u":"glossary.html#sec-2","p":"Web development glossary in plain words","h":"Python and tools","x":"Python A programming language with simple rules. It is used for websites, data analysis and automation. Lessons from scratch . Framework A ready-made set of tools for building a website. With it you do not have to write everything yourself. Framework comparison . Django A big framework. It can do password login, an admin panel and data handling right away. Named after the guitarist Django Reinhardt. Flask A small framework. It gives you only the basics, and everything else is added as needed. Good for a first website. Terminal A window where you give the computer commands as text instead of with the mouse. On Windows it is PowerShell or Command Prompt, on macOS and Linux it is Terminal. pip A program that downloads and installs packages for Python. The command pip install flask installs Flask. Virtual environment (venv) A separate folder with the packages of one project. Packages of different projects do not get in each other's way. Created with the command python -m venv .venv. View A function that receives a request and returns a response. In Flask it is called a route handler, in Django a view. Template An HTML page with gaps that the program fills with data. For example, the ti"},{"u":"glossary.html#sec-3","p":"Web development glossary in plain words","h":"Data","x":"Database A store of records where data is easy to find and change: users, products, orders. Section on databases . SQL A language for querying a database. For example: “show all tasks that are not done yet”. SQLite A simple database stored in a single file. Handy for learning and small projects. ORM A way to work with a database in Python without writing SQL queries by hand. Model A description of a database table in Python: which fields a record has and of what type. For example, a task has a title and a “done” mark. Migration A written instruction for how to change the tables in a database: add a table or a field. In Django, makemigrations creates it and migrate applies it. CRUD The four basic actions with records: create, read, update and delete. Almost every website has them."},{"u":"glossary.html#sec-4","p":"Web development glossary in plain words","h":"Working on a project","x":"API and REST API The way programs talk to each other. For example, a weather app gets data from a server through an API. Git A program that keeps the history of changes in your code. You can go back to any version. Section on Git . Deploy Putting a website on the internet so other people can open it. How to publish a site ."},{"u":"html-css.html","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","x":"Home / HTML and CSS HTML and CSS Five lessons about what any page is made of: tags, links, forms and styling. In the last lesson Python builds the HTML itself. Click “Show” to see the result right away. It is best to start after the “Python from scratch” lessons: the last lesson uses functions and loops. Unfamiliar words are in the glossary . 0 of 5 lessons done"},{"u":"html-css.html#sec-1","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"Lesson 1. What HTML is: tags and the page skeleton","x":"Why. When you open a site, a Python server answers the browser with text in the HTML language. The browser reads this text and draws the page. To make your own site, you need to know how this text is built. What HTML is. It is a markup language, not a program. It has no conditions or loops. HTML simply labels parts of the text: “this is a heading”, “this is a paragraph”, “this is an important word”. Tags. A label is called a tag and is written in angle brackets. Tags usually come in pairs: an opening <p> and a closing </p> with a slash. The text between them is the content. A tag together with its content is called an element. The most useful tags: <h1> … <h6> — headings, <h1> is the main one and usually there is one per page; <p> — a paragraph of text; <strong> — important words, the browser shows them in bold, <em> — emphasis in italics; <br> — a line break. It is a single tag, you don't need to close it. Nesting. Tags are put inside each other like boxes: <p>This is <strong>important</strong></p> . They are closed in reverse order: first the inner one, then the outer one. The page skeleton. Every page has the same frame: the line <!doctype html> , then <html> , inside it <head> "},{"u":"html-css.html#sec-2","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"Lesson 2. Lists, links and images","x":"Why. A site menu is a list of links. A product card is an image with a caption. These three tags appear on almost every page. Lists. <ul> is a bulleted list, <ol> is a numbered one. Each item goes in <li> . It is like a list in Python, only for showing on a page. Links. <a href=\"address\">text</a> . The href attribute holds where the link leads, and between the tags goes the text you click. Attributes are extra information inside the opening tag: name=\"value\" . A link has href , an image has src and alt . Full and short addresses. https://www.python.org leads to another site. about.html leads to a page of this same site, the path is counted from the current page. Images. <img src=\"address\" alt=\"description\"> is a single tag. src is where to get the image. alt is the text instead of the image: screen readers for blind people read it, and it is shown if the image didn't load. Write in it what the image shows. Try it: add your own item with a link to the menu. In the image example change width to 150. Common mistake: an address without https:// The browser will read <a href=\"www.python.org\"> as a page of this same site called “www.python.org” and open a 404 error. Write the address of "},{"u":"html-css.html#sec-3","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"Lesson 3. Forms: how a site receives data","x":"Why. Sign-up, search, a review, an order — all of these are forms. It is through a form that data from a person reaches the server, your Python program. The <form> tag groups fields together. The action attribute says where to send the data, and method says how. Fields. <input> is a single tag for input. Its kind is set by type : text — text, email — email, password — a password shown as dots, number — a number, checkbox — a tick box. For long text there is <textarea> , for sending — <button> . The name attribute matters most. It is the name under which the value reaches the server. In Flask it becomes a dictionary key: request.form[\"email\"] . Without name the field isn't sent at all. The <label> caption. Connect the caption to the field: the field has id=\"email\" , the caption has for=\"email\" . Then clicking the caption puts the cursor in the field, and screen readers read the caption aloud. GET and POST. method=\"get\" puts the data into the page address, this is how search works: ?q=python . method=\"post\" sends it hidden, in the request body: this is how passwords and reviews are sent. Try it: add an age field with type=\"number\" to the form. Sending is turned off in the preview win"},{"u":"html-css.html#sec-4","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"Lesson 4. CSS: how a page looks","x":"Why. HTML says what is on the page: a heading, a paragraph, a button. And CSS says how it looks: colours, sizes, spacing. This very site is styled with CSS. Where to write it. The easiest is in the <style> tag at the top of the page. Big sites move CSS into a separate file. A CSS rule consists of a selector and properties in curly braces: h1 { color: navy; } . It reads: “all h1 are dark blue”. A semicolon goes after each property. Selectors: by tag: p { ... } — all paragraphs; by class: .card { ... } — all elements with class=\"card\" . This is the most common way; by id: #top { ... } — one element with id=\"top\" . Useful properties. color — text colour, background — background, font-size — font size, font-weight: bold — boldness, text-align: center — alignment. padding — spacing inside the border, margin — outside , border — a border, border-radius — rounded corners. Colours are set with a word ( red ) or a code: #ffd249 is this site's yellow. Try it: change the card background to #a4f6f8 and the rounding to 20px . Common mistake: a dot in the class attribute The dot is written only in CSS: .card { ... } . In HTML the class is given without it: class=\"card\" . With a dot, class=\".card"},{"u":"html-css.html#sec-5","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"Lesson 5. How Python builds HTML","x":"Why. On a real site HTML isn't written by hand for every user. It is built by a program on the server: it fills in a name, a task list, prices. This lesson connects everything you know about Python and HTML. HTML is just a string. For Python a page is ordinary text. So it can be built with an f-string: f\"<h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>\" . Lists become a loop. To show a task list, the program goes through it and adds an <li> line for each task. This is how feeds, catalogues and menus are made. Danger: someone else's code in the page. If you insert a user's text into HTML as is, they can write their own tag there, for example <script> , and this code will run for other visitors. This attack is called XSS. The protection is the html.escape function: it turns < into the safe &lt; , and the browser shows the character instead of running the tag. Templates. Gluing big pages together from strings is awkward. So Flask and Django use templates: an HTML file with gaps {{ name }} and loops {% for %} . They call escape for every value themselves. More on the “Templates and forms” page. Try it: in the first example add a task to the list. In the second change the comment text and compare the output wit"},{"u":"html-css.html#next-title","p":"HTML and CSS: the basics for a future web developer","h":"What's next","x":"Now you know what a page is made of. All that's left is to set up your computer and build your first site on Flask or Django. Setting up your computer First project"},{"u":"index.html","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"PyWeb Guide","x":"First site with Python A free learning site for people who have never programmed. You will learn the basics of Python, a simple programming language, and build your own website step by step. Start from scratch I already know Python Change the name and press “Run” Hello, Anna This is a real Python program. It runs right here and writes on this page."},{"u":"index.html#continue-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"Continue learning","x":"You have already started. Here is where you stopped. Python from scratch: Flask: Django: Blog API:"},{"u":"index.html#intro-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"No experience needed","x":"7 lessons How it works You start with one line of code and press a button. The first lessons run right in the browser, so there is nothing to install, and a mistake won't break anything. Then you find out how a website works inside: who receives your request, where the data lives and where the page comes from. After that you build your first website, a to-do list, following ready-made instructions, and put it online. The whole course is free. It was made by a student of North Kazakhstan University."},{"u":"index.html#plan-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"Five steps to your own website","x":"0 of 5 steps done. Your ticks are saved in this browser. 13 lessons Learn the basics of Python How to store data, set conditions and write your own commands. From-scratch lessons . Step done 8 steps Understand how a website works What happens between clicking a link and seeing the page. Step-by-step walkthrough . Step done 1 quiz Pick a tool Two tools suit a first site: Flask and Django. A mini quiz will suggest which one to choose. Step done 1 section Teach your site to remember Records are kept in a database , so they don't disappear. Step done 6 steps Build your own site A simple first project with ready-made instructions . Later you can put it online . Step done"},{"u":"index.html#parts-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"What a website is made of","x":"Four words you will meet most often. Route Connects an address to the right page, the way an operator connected calls. Server The computer that runs the website's program. Pictured: an IBM 704, 1957. Database Keeps records, like cards in a catalogue. Framework A kit for building websites. Django is named after this guitarist. Came across an unfamiliar word? Everything is explained in the glossary ."},{"u":"index.html#inside-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"What's inside","x":"Python from scratch 13 lessons right in the browser, nothing to install Lessons About Python What happens when you open a page Section Frameworks Django, Flask and FastAPI: which to choose Section Databases Where a site keeps records and how to find them Section First project A to-do list with Flask or Django Project Deployment Your site online on PythonAnywhere Section Templates and forms Pages from templates and data validation Section Authentication Passwords, login and access rights Section REST API How programs exchange data Section Testing How to check that everything works Section Git The history of changes in your code Section Second project An API for a blog Project Sandbox Write and run code right away Tool Exercises Ten auto-checked exercises Practice Cheat sheet The essentials on one page Tool Final test Check what you remember Practice"},{"u":"index.html#words-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"Words in plain language","x":"Python Server Backend HTTP HTML CSS JavaScript Framework Django Flask Database SQL ORM API Git Deploy"},{"u":"index.html#ideas-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"What you can build","x":"To-do list Add, delete and tick off tasks: the simplest real website. Step-by-step guide . Mini blog Articles, categories and a separate page for each article. Second project, step by step . Link shortener A long link turns into a short one. Simple logic and a database. An idea to try on your own Portfolio A personal site with your projects, contacts and a good-looking presentation. An idea to try on your own"},{"u":"index.html#faq-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"Frequently asked questions","x":"I have never programmed. Can I do it? Yes. Start with the from-scratch lessons : they need no experience and nothing to install. Is it paid? No, the whole site is free. Do I need to learn Django and Flask at once? No. It's better to pick one framework, build a simple project with it and only then compare it with the other one. Can I build websites without a database? Yes, but most real projects store users, products or content, so a database soon becomes necessary. What matters more: theory or practice? Practice. Theory helps you understand what is going on, but the skill only comes from writing code and working through your mistakes."},{"u":"index.html#sources-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"Sources","x":"The material is checked against the official documentation. Checked on 06.11.2026 . Python 3 documentation : the language and the standard library Django 6.1 documentation : current version 6.1.3 Flask 3.1 documentation : current version 3.1.3 MDN: an overview of HTTP : how the browser talks to the server"},{"u":"index.html#final-title","p":"PyWeb Guide","h":"Start now","x":"No sign-up needed, and the first lesson takes a few minutes. The first lesson is already open Open the first lesson"},{"u":"playground.html","p":"Python sandbox in the browser","h":"Python sandbox in the browser","x":"Home / Sandbox Python right in the browser Write code and press “Run”. Nothing to install: the code runs on your device and is not sent anywhere."},{"u":"playground.html#editor-title","p":"Python sandbox in the browser","h":"Editor","x":"Ctrl+Enter runs the code. Your draft is saved in this browser. Example:"},{"u":"playground.html#limits-title","p":"Python sandbox in the browser","h":"What to know","x":"The sandbox runs on Pyodide: Python built for the browser. On the first run the browser downloads about 5 MB from jsDelivr. The Flask example also loads packages from PyPI. A real server cannot be started here. Instead, the Flask app receives requests through test_client() . input() does not work in the browser. Set the values right in the code. Code stops after 10 seconds, so an infinite loop will not freeze the page."},{"u":"playground.html#sources-title","p":"Python sandbox in the browser","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . Pyodide documentation"},{"u":"practice.html","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","x":"Home / Exercises Exercises with automatic checking Write a solution and press “Check”. The code runs in the browser and goes through hidden tests. If a test fails, you will see what it checked, what was expected and what came out. Stuck? Open a hint. Solved exercises are remembered in this browser."},{"u":"practice.html#result-title","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"Your result","x":"The first check loads Python: about 5 MB, which takes a few seconds. 0 of 40 exercises solved"},{"u":"practice.html#basics-title","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"Python basics","x":"Functions, strings and dictionaries: every request handler is built on them. 1. Greeting Level: Easy Write a function greet(name) that returns the string Hello, Name! . Extra spaces around the name must be removed. If the name is empty, return Hello, guest! . Hint 1. The easiest way to join text and a variable is an f-string: f\"Hello, {name}!\" . Hint 2. name.strip() removes the spaces at both ends. Hint 3. An empty string counts as false in a condition: name or 'guest' gives “guest” when the name is empty. Show solution 2. Word count Level: Easy Write count_words(text) : return a dictionary where the key is a word in lower case and the value is how many times it appears. Hint 1. Start with an empty dictionary counts = {} and loop over the words. Hint 2. text.lower().split() lowercases the text and splits it on any spaces and line breaks. Hint 3. counts.get(word, 0) + 1 increases the counter even when the word is not in the dictionary yet. Show solution 3. Strong password Level: Easy Write is_strong(password) . A password is strong if it has at least 8 characters, at least one digit and at least one letter. Return True or False . Hint 1. len(password) gives the length of the string."},{"u":"practice.html#http-title","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"HTTP and routes","x":"What a framework does for you is easy to write in a simple form. 11. Parsing an address Level: Medium Write parse_query(query) . It turns the string a=1&b=2 into the dictionary {\"a\": \"1\", \"b\": \"2\"} . Skip parts without an = sign. A value may contain = . Hint 1. First split the string into pairs: query.split(\"&\") . Hint 2. Skip a pair without = with the check if \"=\" in part . Hint 3. part.split(\"=\", 1) splits only on the first = , the rest stay in the value. Show solution 12. A simple router Level: Medium Finish handle(method, path) . It looks up a handler in the ROUTES dictionary and returns a “code, text” pair. If there is no such address, return (404, \"Not Found\") . If the address exists but the method does not match, return (405, \"Method Not Allowed\") . Hint 1. Check in order: first whether the address exists, then whether the method does. Hint 2. path not in ROUTES means there is no such address: that is 404. Hint 3. The address's methods are in ROUTES[path] . If the method is not there, it is 405; otherwise return 200 and the text. Show solution 13. Checking form data Level: Hard Write validate_user(data) . The function takes a dictionary and returns a list of errors. The name"},{"u":"practice.html#forms-title","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"Forms and data checks","x":"The server checks everything a user sends on its own. 22. Clean title Level: Easy Write clean_title(value) : remove the spaces at both ends and replace several spaces in a row inside with one. \" Buy bread \" must become Buy bread . Hint 1. value.split() without arguments splits on any whitespace and drops empty parts. Hint 2. Put the words back together with \" \".join(...) . Show solution 23. A number from a form Level: Medium Form fields always arrive as strings. Write to_int(value, default) : turn the string into a whole number. Spaces at the ends are fine. If the string is not a number, return default . Hint 1. int(\"abc\") raises ValueError . A try / except ValueError block catches it. Hint 2. In the except block simply return the fallback: return default . Show solution 24. Required fields Level: Medium Write missing_fields(data, required) . data is a dictionary with form data, required is a list of required fields. Return the list of fields that are missing from the dictionary or empty. A string of spaces is empty too. Keep the order of required . Hint 1. data.get(name, \"\") returns an empty string when the field is missing. Hint 2. not value.strip() checks for emptiness: nothing "},{"u":"practice.html#sql-title","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"SQL","x":"A real SQLite database runs right in the browser. 27. Safe insert Level: Medium Write add_task(db, title) : it adds a row to the tasks table and returns its id . Use the ? parameter: text with a quote must not break the query. Hint 1. The insert query: INSERT INTO tasks (title) VALUES (?) . Hint 2. The values are passed as the second argument, a tuple: db.execute(query, (title,)) . The comma after title is required. Hint 3. execute returns a cursor, and the new row's number is in cursor.lastrowid . Do not forget db.commit() . Show solution 28. Selecting with a condition Level: Easy Write active_titles(db) : return the list of titles with done = 0 , sorted alphabetically. Hint 1. You need the query SELECT title FROM tasks with the condition WHERE done = 0 . Hint 2. ORDER BY title at the end of the query sorts alphabetically. Hint 3. Each result row is a tuple with the title first: [row[0] for row in rows] . Show solution 29. How many are left Level: Easy Write count_active(db) : return the number of tasks where done = 0 . The database must do the counting, not Python. Hint 1. The query SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tasks WHERE done = 0 counts rows. Hint 2. .fetchone() returns one row as a tu"},{"u":"practice.html#flask-title","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"Flask","x":"Real Flask. The first check also downloads the package from PyPI. 33. The first route Level: Easy Add a route GET /hello/<name> that returns the JSON {\"message\": \"Hello, name!\"} . Hint 1. A route is declared with a decorator above the function: @app.get(\"/hello/<name>\") . Hint 2. The name in the angle brackets of the address and the function's argument must match: def hello(name): . Hint 3. If you return a dictionary, Flask turns it into JSON itself: return {\"message\": f\"Hello, {name}!\"} . Show solution 34. A filter in the address Level: Medium Make GET /tasks that returns the JSON list of tasks from TASKS . If the address has ?done=1 , return only the finished ones; with ?done=0 , only the unfinished ones. Without the parameter, return them all. Hint 1. request.args.get(\"done\") reads the parameter from the address. If it is missing, you get None . Hint 2. The parameter arrives as a string: compare it with \"1\" , not with a number. Hint 3. If you return a list of dictionaries from the function, Flask turns it into JSON itself. Show solution 35. Creating a record Level: Hard Make POST /items . It reads JSON with a title field. If the field is missing or empty, return {\"error\": \"title"},{"u":"practice.html#sec-7","p":"Python and web development exercises with automatic checking","h":"Ready for more?","x":"Build a blog API with a database and tests. Open the section"},{"u":"privacy.html","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Privacy policy","x":"Home / Privacy policy Privacy policy In short: the site has no database of its own. Messages sent through the form are published on GitHub and visible to everyone, and the hosting records visitors' IP addresses. The separate version with an account has a server, and what it stores is described below."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-1","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Who is responsible for the site","x":"PyWeb Guide is a learning project. Author: E. Zhanguzhin You can contact him through the Issues section on GitHub ; messages there are public, so do not include personal data in them. Without a GitHub account you can write directly : only the author sees such a message."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-2","p":"Privacy policy","h":"What is stored in your browser","x":"The site uses localStorage, your browser's storage. These records are not sent to the server, and the author cannot see them: pyweb_messages — copies of messages from the form on the “Contact” page; pyweb_progress — the learning steps you ticked; pyweb_theme — the chosen colour theme; pyweb_tasks and pyweb_task_* — solved exercises and solution drafts; pyweb_playground — your code draft in the sandbox; pyweb_exam — your best final test result; pyweb_os — which system (Windows or macOS and Linux) you chose in the command tabs. The “Clear saved messages” button on the “Contact” page deletes the messages. Everything else, including the cache, is deleted by clearing this site's data in your browser settings. In addition, the browser keeps copies of pages, styles, scripts and Python files in its cache (Cache Storage) so the site opens faster and without the internet. These are ordinary site files with none of your data in them. The progress file from the “My progress” page is created right in the browser and contains only your marks. The name for the certificate is not stored: the printable sheet is assembled in the browser and not sent anywhere."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-3","p":"Privacy policy","h":"What happens when you send the form","x":"The form opens the page for creating an entry in GitHub Issues with your name and text. Nothing is published until you confirm it on GitHub. After that the message and your GitHub profile are visible to everyone, and GitHub handles them under its own rules."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-4","p":"Privacy policy","h":"What the hosting records","x":"The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. According to GitHub, the visitor's IP address is logged and stored for security purposes. Source: GitHub Pages help ; details are in the GitHub privacy statement ."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-5","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Version with an account","x":"You can also take the course in a separate version with an account at pywebguide.pythonanywhere.com . An account is only needed to carry your progress between devices: the course works the same without it. This version stores on its server: your username and password, if you signed up. The password is stored only as a hash, and nobody can read it, including the author; your progress: completed steps, solved exercises and your best final test score. They are saved only while you are logged in; messages from the form on the Contact page: name, text, date and your account, if you are logged in. Only the author sees them; they are never published; failed login attempts: username, IP address and browser. This protects against password guessing: after five mistakes, login is blocked for an hour, and a successful login resets the counter. Successful logins are not recorded. To limit the number of messages from the form, the server remembers for one hour how many came from your IP address. This number is kept only in the server's memory, not in the database. This version sets two technical cookies: sessionid to remember your login (for up to two weeks) and csrftoken to protect forms from f"},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-6","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Running Python in the browser","x":"Only after you press a run or check button does the browser load Python (Pyodide) from jsDelivr, and for Flask examples also packages from PyPI. Like any website, these services receive your IP address and browser data. The code itself runs on your device and is not sent anywhere. The name for the certificate also stays in the browser and is not stored."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-7","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Cookies and visit statistics","x":"The site sets no cookies and shows no ads. The technical cookies of the version with an account are described above . To understand which pages are read and where people drop the course, the site counts visits with GoatCounter . It uses no cookies and does not recognise you on later visits. It records the address and title of the page you open, the site you came from, the country based on your IP address, the screen size, browser and operating system. The IP address itself is not stored. Details are in the GoatCounter policy . The site counts learning events in the same way: a lesson or step is marked as done, code is run for the first time, an exercise is solved. They contain no names or answers, and the same details are sent as when a page is opened. To avoid counting an event twice, the list of events already sent is kept in your browser (key pyweb_events). If Do Not Track or an ad blocker is on in your browser, the visit may not be counted, and the site works just the same."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-8","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Site search","x":"Search runs in your browser: the index is loaded from this same site, and your queries are not sent anywhere."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-9","p":"Privacy policy","h":"External links","x":"The site has links to documentation and other external resources. Once you follow them, those resources' rules apply."},{"u":"privacy.html#sec-10","p":"Privacy policy","h":"Changes","x":"If the policy changes, we will update this text and the date at the end of the page. In effect since 3 ноября 2026 года . Updated on 13 ноября 2026 года : added a section about the version with an account."},{"u":"progress.html","p":"My progress","h":"My progress","x":"Home / My progress My progress All your marks in one place: lessons, project steps, exercises and the final test. Progress is kept only in this browser, but you can move it to another device with a file."},{"u":"progress.html#dash-title","p":"My progress","h":"Summary","x":"Tick “Step done” and solve exercises: this is where you will see how much is done. The summary needs JavaScript."},{"u":"progress.html#transfer-title","p":"My progress","h":"Move to another device","x":"Download the file here and upload it on this same page on your phone or another computer. Load from file The file contains only progress marks, no personal data. Loading it replaces your current progress."},{"u":"progress.html#cert-title","p":"My progress","h":"Certificate","x":"The certificate opens when you score at least 12 out of 15 in the final test . Name for the certificate In the print window, choose a printer or “Save as PDF”. The name is not sent anywhere. This is a learning certificate for self-assessment, not an official document."},{"u":"project.html","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"First project with Flask and Django","x":"Home / First project First project: a to-do list We will build the same app — a to-do list — in two ways: with Flask and with Django. Pick one option and follow it to the end. Each has two parts: first the simplest list, then a database, deleting and a “done” mark. Flask takes about 30 minutes, Django about an hour. Just starting out? First go through the “From scratch” lessons , and look up unfamiliar words in the glossary . Flask option Django option What you need Flask: 6 steps Django: 10 steps Common errors Sources"},{"u":"project.html#prep-title","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"What you need","x":"Check this before you start, and the steps will go without errors. Don't know how to install Python and open a terminal? First go through “Setting up your computer” . Python 3.12 or newer: the command python --version in the terminal. A code editor, for example VS Code. A separate empty folder for the project, opened in the terminal. Basic Python: functions, lists, dictionaries (step 1 of the roadmap ). The commands are for Windows. On macOS and Linux type python3 instead of python , and activate the virtual environment with source .venv/bin/activate . Your ticked steps are saved in this browser, so you can come back later and continue. If something goes wrong, see the common errors at the bottom of the page."},{"u":"project.html#flask-title","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"Option 1. A to-do list with Flask","x":"Flask 3.1, about 30 minutes. One app in one file: a page with the list and a form for adding a task. Download the full code (zip, 3 KB) View on GitHub 0 of 6 steps done Step 1. Create an environment and install Flask A virtual environment keeps the project's libraries separate from the rest of the system. The commands differ slightly between Windows and macOS or Linux: choose your system, and the site will remember it. Windows (PowerShell or Command Prompt) Terminal macOS and Linux (Terminal) Terminal Step done Step 2. Write the app Create the file app.py . For now the tasks are kept in an ordinary list, in the server's memory. app.py Step done Step 3. Start the server The --debug flag restarts the server every time you save the file and shows clear error messages. Terminal Step done Step 4. Check the result Open http://127.0.0.1:5000/ in the browser and add a couple of tasks. Note that the list is cleared after the server restarts: the data lived only in memory. Keeping it between runs is what a database does, and that is the next step. What it should look like Two tasks added through the form. There is no styling yet: this is plain HTML without CSS. Step done Part 2. Database and"},{"u":"project.html#django-title","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"Option 2. A to-do list with Django","x":"Django 6.1, about 60 minutes. The tasks are stored in an SQLite database, and you can add them through the built-in admin panel. Download the full code (zip, 8 KB) View on GitHub 0 of 10 steps done Step 1. Install Django and create a project The dot at the end of the command creates the project in the current folder. Then we create the tasks app. Windows (PowerShell or Command Prompt) Terminal macOS and Linux (Terminal) Terminal Step done Step 2. Connect the app In mysite/settings.py , add the line 'tasks', to the end of the INSTALLED_APPS list. mysite/settings.py Step done Step 3. Describe the model and register it in the admin A model is a database table described as a Python class. The ORM creates the SQL for it. tasks/models.py tasks/admin.py Step done Step 4. Create the tables and an administrator Migrations carry the models over into the database. The last command asks for a username and password for the admin. Terminal Step done Step 5. Make the list page The view gets the tasks from the database, the template shows them as a list, and the route links the address to the view. Put the template in the tasks/templates/tasks/ folder, and Django will find it. tasks/views.py tasks"},{"u":"project.html#errors-title","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"Common errors and how to fix them","x":"The messages are shown as Windows, Flask and Django print them. Click an error to see the fix. The python command opens the Microsoft Store or says “Python was not found” Python was not found; run without arguments to install from the Microsoft Store… Python is not installed or not on PATH, so Windows shows its own stub. Install Python from python.org and tick “Add python.exe to PATH”. If Python is already installed, use the py command: py -m venv .venv . Once the environment is activated, the python command works. PowerShell will not activate the environment: “running scripts is disabled” …Activate.ps1 cannot be loaded because running scripts is disabled on this system. On Russian-language Windows the same error reads: Невозможно загрузить файл …\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1, так как выполнение сценариев отключено в этой системе. Allow local scripts for your account, once: Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned . Another option is to open Command Prompt (cmd) instead of PowerShell. “flask” or “django-admin” is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet flask : The term 'flask' is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet, function, script file, or operable program. The environ"},{"u":"project.html#sources-title","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"Sources","x":"The code is written for Flask 3.1 and Django 6.1, sources checked on 06.11.2026 . Flask: installation and virtual environment Flask: Quickstart — routes, forms, templates Django 6.1: official tutorial, part 1 — project, app, routes Django 6.1: tutorial, part 2 — models, migrations, admin Django 6.1: ModelForm — forms based on models Django 6.1: CSRF protection — why csrf_token is needed Python: the sqlite3 module — query parameters and connecting Python: the venv module — virtual environments"},{"u":"project.html#sec-6","p":"First project with Flask and Django","h":"Didn't work?","x":"Describe the step where the error appeared through the feedback form, and compare with the sources above. Ask a question"},{"u":"project2.html","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","x":"Home / Second project Second project: a blog API We will build an API for a blog with Flask and SQLite, with data validation, tests and a simple JavaScript page. You will need what you learned in the databases and REST API sections. It takes about an hour."},{"u":"project2.html#plan-title","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"What you will get","x":"All the code fits into a single app.py file. You can run the last example right on this page. 0 of 7 steps done"},{"u":"project2.html#steps-title","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"Project steps","x":"Tick off what you have done: your progress is saved in the browser. Step 1. Create the project A new folder with its own environment, as in the first project. Terminal On macOS and Linux the environment is activated with source .venv/bin/activate . Step done Step 2. Connect the database The connection opens for the duration of a request and closes at the end. The table is created on the first run if it does not exist yet. The create_app function lets tests use a separate database. app.py Step done Step 3. Add data validation The function returns either clean data or an error text. Put it above create_app so that it is easy to test on its own. app.py Step done Step 4. Make the list and creating a record Values are passed through ? marks, so the database protects the query from SQL injection itself. Never put user data into SQL with an f-string. app.py Step done Step 5. Make reading, changing and deleting If there is no such record, we return a 404 error instead of crashing. app.py Running on your computer: flask --app \"app:create_app()\" run --debug . Step done Step 6. Write tests Run them with the pytest command. The tmp_path fixture gives a temporary folder, so the tests do not tou"},{"u":"project2.html#whole-title","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"All the code and running it","x":"Here is the whole app.py file and a short check at the end. The database is created in a temporary file. app.py in full"},{"u":"project2.html#further-title","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"What to add on your own","x":"These ideas reinforce what you learned in other sections. Pagination: the ?limit=10&offset=20 parameters and the SQL LIMIT ? OFFSET ? . Author login: posts can be created and deleted only after authentication . Search by title with LIKE ? . Deployment to PythonAnywhere: put application = create_app(\"/home/username/blog-api/blog.db\") in the WSGI file."},{"u":"project2.html#sources-title","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . Flask: SQLite Flask: application factories Python: sqlite3 query placeholders"},{"u":"project2.html#sec-6","p":"Second project: a blog API with Flask and SQLite","h":"Test yourself","x":"Take the final test and get a learning certificate. Open the section"},{"u":"setup.html","p":"Setting up your computer: Python, VS Code and the terminal","h":"Setting up your computer: Python, VS Code and the terminal","x":"Home / Setting up your computer Setting up your computer Two lessons before your first project: how to install Python and a code editor, and how to stop being afraid of the terminal. Commands are given for Windows and for macOS with Linux, pick your system. If something doesn't work, check the common errors of the first project. Unfamiliar words are in the glossary . 0 of 2 lessons done"},{"u":"setup.html#sec-1","p":"Setting up your computer: Python, VS Code and the terminal","h":"Lesson 1. Installing Python and a code editor","x":"Why. In the lessons, code ran right in the browser. But your own Flask or Django site runs on your own computer. For that you need two programs: Python itself and an editor that makes writing code comfortable. Python. Download the installer from the official site python.org , version 3.12 or newer. Download it only from there: other sites may slip you a virus disguised as Python. The most important thing on Windows. On the first screen of the installer, tick “Add python.exe to PATH” , then click “Install Now”. Without this tick the python command in the terminal will not work. On macOS install Python with the installer from the same site. On Linux it is usually already there: check with the command below. Check the installation. Open a terminal (how to do it is in the next lesson) and run the command from your system's tab. You should see a version, for example Python 3.13.1 . Code editor. A handy free editor is Visual Studio Code . After installing it, open the extensions section (the icon of four squares on the left) and install the Python extension by Microsoft. It highlights code and points out errors. Your first program on the computer. Create an empty folder, for example pywe"},{"u":"setup.html#sec-2","p":"Setting up your computer: Python, VS Code and the terminal","h":"Lesson 2. The terminal without fear","x":"Why. Programmers often control the computer not with the mouse but with commands: they install libraries, start the server, create projects. All of this is done in the terminal . You will need it from the very first step of the first project. What a terminal is. It is a window where you type a command in words and press Enter, and the computer runs it and prints an answer. It is very hard to break the computer with it by accident: the commands in this course are safe. How to open it. The easiest way is right in VS Code: the “Terminal → New Terminal” menu or the keys Ctrl + ` . Separately: on Windows press “Start” and type “PowerShell”, on macOS open the “Terminal” app. Where you are. The terminal always works in some folder, and its path is written at the start of the line. Commands run right there. If a program “can't find the file”, most often you are simply in the wrong folder. Five main commands: cd folder — go into a folder, cd .. — go one level up; ls (in the old Windows command prompt — dir ) — show the files in the folder; mkdir name — create a folder; python hello.py — run a program (on macOS — python3 ); Ctrl + C — stop a running program, for example a server. Two tips th"},{"u":"setup.html#next-title","p":"Setting up your computer: Python, VS Code and the terminal","h":"What's next","x":"Your computer is ready. Now you can build your first site: a to-do list on Flask or Django. First project HTML and CSS"},{"u":"start.html","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","x":"Home / From scratch Python from scratch 13 lessons for people who have never programmed. The code runs right on this page: click “Run”, change it and run it again. Making mistakes is not scary, nothing will break. On the first run the browser loads Python, which takes a few seconds. Unfamiliar words are in the glossary (in Russian for now). 0 of 17 lessons and projects done"},{"u":"start.html#sec-1","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 0. How this course works","x":"Why. Before writing code, it helps to understand three things: what a program is, why we learn Python and how to study on this page. What a program is. A program is an instruction for a computer. It is like a recipe: a cook follows the steps in order, first chopping, then cooking. A computer also runs commands one by one, from top to bottom. Why Python. Python code reads almost like ordinary English text, with few extra symbols. People use Python to build websites, process data and automate boring work. That is why it is often chosen as a first language. How to study. Every lesson has an explanation, examples and exercises. Click “Run” under an example, and Python will run the code right here, in the browser. You can change the code however you like: the computer won't break. Exercises with checking. Write a solution and click “Check”. If it doesn't work out, open the hints one by one. There is a ready solution too, but try on your own first. Progress. The “Lesson done” tick is saved in this browser. Look up unfamiliar words in the glossary . Try it: run both examples of this lesson. They are already real programs. Common question: do I need to install anything? No. Python loads in"},{"u":"start.html#sec-2","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 1. Your first program","x":"Why. Any program, even a big website, is made of simple commands. Let's start with the most important one — the command that shows a result. The print command shows on the screen what is in the brackets. Text is written in quotes: print(\"Hello!\") . Numbers can be written without quotes: print(2026) . Order. Each command is written on a new line. Python runs them from top to bottom: what is higher gets printed earlier. Several values. One print can list several values separated by commas. Python puts spaces between them itself: print(\"I am\", 16, \"years old\") prints I am 16 years old . An empty line. print() with nothing in the brackets prints an empty line. It is handy for separating one part of the output from another. Comments. Everything after the # sign is a note for people. Python skips it. Comments help explain what the code does. Try it: write your name in quotes and click “Run”. Then swap the two lines and see how the output changes. Common mistake: a missing quote or bracket Quotes and brackets always come in pairs. If you write print(\"Hello!) , Python can't find the end of the text: SyntaxError: unterminated string literal Check that every opening quote and bracket has a c"},{"u":"start.html#sec-3","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 2. Numbers: Python as a calculator","x":"Why. Websites count all the time: the price in a cart, test scores, how many days are left until an exam. So first let's learn to count. Operations. Python adds + , subtracts - , multiplies * and divides / . You can print the result right away: print(2 + 3) . Whole and decimal numbers. There are whole numbers, for example 7 , and decimal ones, for example 7.5 . The fractional part is separated with a dot , not a comma. Ordinary division / always gives a decimal number: 10 / 2 equals 5.0 . Whole division and remainder. Imagine 17 sweets packed 5 to a box. 17 // 5 answers how many full boxes you get: 3 . 17 % 5 answers how many sweets are left over: 2 . Powers and order of operations. 2 ** 3 is 2 cubed, that is 8 . The order of operations is as in maths: first multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction. Brackets change the order: (2 + 3) * 4 equals 20 . Rounding. round(3.14159, 2) keeps two digits after the dot: 3.14 . A number and text are different things. 2 + 2 gives 4 , but \"2\" + \"2\" gives \"22\" : that is glued text, not a sum. Try it: calculate how many minutes there are in a week. Then check how the remainder works: what does 10 % 3 give? Common mistake: a comma "},{"u":"start.html#sec-4","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 3. Variables: how to remember a value","x":"Why. A program has to remember data: a student's name, test scores, the total in a cart. That is what variables are for. What a variable is. A variable is a labelled box. You put a value in the box and then refer to it by the label. name = \"Anya\" means: put the text \"Anya\" in the box labelled name . The = sign in Python means “put”, not “equals”. The box name goes on the left, and what goes into it on the right. A value can change. score = score + 10 means: take what is in score , add 10 and put it back. The short way to write the same thing is score += 10 . How to name variables. Use Latin letters, digits and the _ sign. A name can't start with a digit. Choose clear names: user_age is better than a . Value types. Every value has a type: str — text, int — a whole number, float — a decimal. type(...) tells you the type. int(\"16\") , str(16) and float(\"2.5\") turn one into another. Keyboard input. On a computer input(\"What is your name? \") asks a person and waits for an answer. The answer always comes as text, so a number is read like this: int(input()) . In the browser on this site input() doesn't work, so we write values right in the code. And on websites data comes from forms, which"},{"u":"start.html#sec-5","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 4. Strings: working with text","x":"Why. Almost everything on a website is text: a student's name, a heading, a message in a form. To work with text, you need to be able to store it, join it and fix it. What a string is. In Python text is called a string . It is letters standing in a row, like beads on a thread. A string is written in quotes: \"Hello\" . Quotes are the borders of the text. Without them Python will think that Hello is a variable name and report an error. Joining strings is done with the + sign: \"Hello, \" + \"Aruzhan\" gives Hello, Aruzhan . Put the space in yourself: Python won't add it. An f-string is the handiest way to insert a value into text. Imagine a postcard with a gap for a name. The letter f before the quotes tells Python: “there are gaps here”. The gaps themselves are curly braces: f\"Hello, {name}!\" . Python fills in the value of the variable. Methods are commands that every string has. They are written after a dot: .strip() removes extra spaces at the start and at the end; .title() makes the first letter capital: aruzhan → Aruzhan ; .upper() makes all letters capital, .lower() — small. Length and letters by number. len(name) counts how many characters a string has. And you can take one letter "},{"u":"start.html#sec-6","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 5. Conditions: if this, then that","x":"Why. A website keeps choosing: whether to let a user in, whether to accept an answer, whether there is enough money for a purchase. Conditions are for choosing. The word if means “if”. After it come the condition and a colon. The lines below it, indented by four spaces, run only if the condition is true. The word else means “otherwise”. Its lines run if the condition is false. And elif means “and if not, check this”. You can write as many such checks as you like, Python goes through them from top to bottom and stops at the first true one. Indentation shows which lines belong to the condition. It is not decoration: without it Python won't understand what to run. Comparisons. > greater, < less, >= and <= — greater than or equal and less than or equal. Equality is checked with two signs: == . Not equal — != . Text can be compared too: password == \"qwerty\" . True and false. The answer to a comparison is True or False . Check it yourself: print(5 > 3) prints True . Several conditions. and is true when both are true. or needs just one. not flips the answer. Try it: in the first example set the temperature to 10, then 30. In the second change the age and the parents' permission. Common mi"},{"u":"start.html#sec-7","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Mini-project 1. A discount calculator","x":"What we'll build. A program for a shop till: it calculates the discount and prints a receipt. This is how the cart of any online shop works, only there the server does the same thing. You'll need: numbers ( lesson 2 ), variables ( lesson 3 ), f-strings ( lesson 4 ) and conditions ( lesson 5 ). The shop's rules: if the purchase is 5000 tenge or more, the discount is 10%; students get another 5% on top; discounts add up: a student buying for 6000 gets 15%. Step-by-step plan: create a variable discount = 0 — the discount in percent; with two separate if s add 10 and 5 to it if the conditions are true; calculate the amount to pay and round it to a whole number: round(...) ; print a three-line receipt as in the “How it should look” example. Why two separate if s and not elif . elif only fires if the previous condition is false. Here the discounts must add up, so each condition is checked on its own. What you can add: free delivery from 10 000 tenge, a 20% birthday discount, a line “You saved: … tenge”. How it should look Project: a receipt with a discount Level: Medium A purchase of 6000 tenge, the customer is a student. Calculate the discount by the shop's rules and print the receipt e"},{"u":"start.html#sec-8","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 6. Lists: many values in one variable","x":"Why. On websites almost everything is a list: a news feed, the students of a class, items in a cart. Keeping each value in its own variable is awkward, so they are collected in a list. What a list is. A list is a row of values in square brackets separated by commas: [\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"] . Imagine a train: each value rides in its own carriage. Carriage numbers start from zero, like the letters in a string. fruits[0] is the first element, fruits[1] is the second. From the end you count with negative numbers: fruits[-1] is the last. How many elements there are is shown by the familiar len(fruits) . A list can change. fruits[1] = \"kiwi\" replaces the second element. .append(\"banana\") adds to the end, .remove(\"pear\") removes by value. Is it in the list? The word in checks this: \"plum\" in fruits gives True or False . Handy in conditions. Lists of numbers. sum() adds up all the numbers, min() and max() find the smallest and largest, sorted() returns them in order. Try it: add your own product to the first list and remove another one. In the second example add one more grade. Common mistake: numbers start from zero In a list of three elements the numbers are 0, 1 and 2. There is no ele"},{"u":"start.html#sec-9","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 7. Loops: one action many times","x":"Why. A website shows a feed of a hundred posts, but the code for one post is written once. A loop repeats it for each one. The for loop means “for each”. for fruit in fruits: reads: “for each fruit in the list of fruits do…”. Python takes the elements one by one, puts the current one into the fruit variable and runs the indented lines. Repeating several times is done with range . range(5) gives the numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — five of them, starting from zero. range(1, 6) gives the numbers from 1 to 5: the last number is not included. Accumulating. A common trick: create a variable before the loop and change it inside. For example, total = 0 , and in the loop total += price . After the loop total holds the sum. The while loop means “while”. It repeats lines while the condition is true. It is needed when you don't know in advance how many repeats there will be: for example, saving until you have the amount you need. To leave early use the break command: the loop ends at once. Try it: in the first example add your own fruit and change range(1, 4) to range(1, 11) . In the second change the amount you save each week. Common mistake: an endless loop If nothing changes inside while , the cond"},{"u":"start.html#sec-10","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 8. Functions: your own command","x":"Why. The same action is often needed in different places: calculating a discounted price, greeting a user. To avoid copying code, you give it a name and call it by that name. What a function is. A function is your own command. You have already used ready-made ones: print , len , round . Now let's learn to make our own. How to define one. The word def starts the definition. Then come the function name, brackets and a colon: def greet(name): . The function's lines are indented, as in conditions and loops. Parameters are the empty places in the brackets. When the function is called, they are filled with values: greet(\"Anya\") puts \"Anya\" into name . Defining is not running. The code inside def works only when the function is called: the name and brackets, greet(\"Anya\") . return hands back the result. A function is like a coffee machine: you give it beans, it gives back coffee. return means “here is the finished result, take it”. You can save it in a variable or use it further: total = price_with_discount(1000, 10) . print and return are different things. print only shows a value on the screen. return hands it back to the program so you can keep working with it. A default value. def gre"},{"u":"start.html#sec-11","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Mini-project 2. A shopping cart","x":"What we'll build. Three functions for an online shop cart. The cart holds the prices of the items, and the functions calculate the total, find expensive items and apply a discount. On a real website the same functions would be called every time the cart is opened. You'll need: lists ( lesson 6 ), loops ( lesson 7 ), conditions ( lesson 5 ) and functions ( lesson 8 ). Three functions: total(cart) returns the sum of all prices. For an empty cart — 0 ; count_expensive(cart, limit) returns how many items cost more than limit ; apply_discount(cart, percent) returns a new list of discounted prices, each rounded to a whole number. Step-by-step plan: total : create s = 0 , add each price in a loop, return s . Yes, there is a ready sum() , but this way you'll understand how it works; count_expensive : the same counter, only add 1 and only under the condition if price > limit ; apply_discount : create an empty list new = [] , in a loop add the discounted price to it with .append() , return new . Why a new list. If you change the original one, the old prices are lost, and websites often show them crossed out next to the new ones. What you can add: a function cheapest(cart) that returns the lo"},{"u":"start.html#sec-12","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 9. Dictionaries: find a value by its name","x":"Why. A website user has a name, an age, a city. It is convenient to store them not by number, as in a list, but by name: “name — Aruzhan”, “city — Almaty”. That is what dictionaries are for. What a dictionary is. A dictionary stores “key — value” pairs in curly braces: {\"bread\": 200, \"milk\": 450} . It is like the contacts in a phone: you find the number by the name. Or like a price list: the price by the product name. To get a value use its key in square brackets: prices[\"bread\"] gives 200 . To add or change: prices[\"cheese\"] = 1500 . If the key wasn't there, it appears, and if it was, the value is replaced. To delete a pair: del prices[\"bread\"] . To get safely. prices.get(\"coffee\", 0) returns the price of coffee, and if it isn't in the dictionary, returns 0 instead of an error. You can check whether a key exists with the familiar word in : \"bread\" in prices . To go through a dictionary. for name, price in prices.items(): takes the pairs in turn: the key goes into name , the value into price . A list of dictionaries is the usual way to store many similar records: products, students, messages. This is exactly the form in which websites pass data to each other, it is called JSON. Try"},{"u":"start.html#sec-13","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Mini-project 3. A quiz","x":"What we'll build. A quiz checker: the questions with correct answers are in one dictionary, the student's answers in another. The program counts the correct answers and gives a grade. Tests on any learning website work like this, including the final test on this site. You'll need: strings ( lesson 4 ), conditions ( lesson 5 ), loops ( lesson 7 ), functions ( lesson 8 ) and dictionaries ( lesson 9 ). Two functions: check_quiz(questions, answers) returns how many answers are correct. An answer counts even if the student wrote it in small letters or with spaces at the edges. If a question wasn't answered, it simply doesn't count; grade(score, total) turns the result into a word: from 90% — excellent , from 70% — good , from 50% — not bad , otherwise try again . Step-by-step plan: in check_quiz create a counter and go through the questions: for question, right in questions.items(): ; get the student's answer safely: answers.get(question, \"\") . If there is no answer, you get an empty string, and the program won't fail; compare the answers after bringing both to the same form: .strip().lower() ; in grade calculate the percent: score * 100 // total , then if and elif , as in the lesson on"},{"u":"start.html#sec-14","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 10. Errors: how to read and catch them","x":"Why. Everyone makes errors, even experienced programmers. A user types letters instead of a number, a record is missing, the internet goes down. A good program doesn't “crash” in such cases, it calmly explains what is wrong. How to read an error message. Python writes several lines, and the most important is the last one. It has the error type and a short explanation, for example ValueError: invalid literal for int() . The line with line 3 shows where to look. On this site a “What it means” hint also appears under the error. Errors you have already met: SyntaxError — the code is written incorrectly: a missing quote, bracket, colon; NameError — there is no such name, often a typo; TypeError — the operation doesn't fit the type, for example text plus a number; IndexError and KeyError — there is no element with such a number or key; ValueError — the value doesn't fit: int(\"abc\") . To catch an error use try and except . It means: “try to do this, and if such an error happens, do that”. The program won't stop. Catch a specific error: except ValueError: , not everything at once. Otherwise except will hide a real typo too, and you'll spend a long time figuring out why the program misbehav"},{"u":"start.html#sec-15","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 11. Modules: ready-made tools","x":"Why. You don't have to write everything yourself. Python comes with a whole library of ready-made tools: random numbers, maths, dates. These sets are called modules . How to connect one. A module is connected with the import command at the start of the program: import random . After that its tools are available after a dot: random.randint(1, 6) . To take one thing write: from datetime import date . Then you just write date , without the module name. Three useful modules: random — chance: randint(1, 6) rolls a die, choice(list) picks a random element; math — maths: sqrt(16) is a square root, pi is the number π; datetime — dates: date.today() is today's date, and the difference of two dates shows how many days are between them. Modules from the internet. Besides the built-in ones, there are thousands of modules by other people, called packages. They are installed with the pip install command. This is how Flask and Django are installed, we'll cover it in the first project. Try it: run the first example several times: the answers will differ. In the second change the event date to your birthday. Common mistake: forgetting import If you write random.randint(1, 6) right away without conn"},{"u":"start.html#sec-16","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Mini-project 4. Guess the number","x":"What we'll build. The computer picks a number from 1 to 100 and then guesses it itself. The trick is that it will guess in 7 tries or fewer , whatever number was picked. This is a real algorithm, called halving or binary search. This is how searching in databases works, and even the “hot and cold” game. You'll need: conditions ( lesson 5 ), the while loop ( lesson 7 ), functions ( lesson 8 ) and the random module ( lesson 11 ). How to guess. The number is somewhere between low = 1 and high = 100 . We name the middle: (low + high) // 2 . If we guessed it, we're done. If the secret is bigger, everything below the middle can be thrown away: low = middle + 1 . If it is smaller, we throw away the upper half: high = middle - 1 . Each try halves the range, so 100 numbers run out very quickly. Step-by-step plan: in the function guess_number(secret, low=1, high=100) create a try counter tries = 0 ; make a while True: loop — “repeat until we say stop”; in the loop add a try and name the middle; if you guessed it, return the number of tries at once: return ends both the loop and the function. Otherwise move low or high . What you can add: printing each try (“trying 50 — higher”), and also a c"},{"u":"start.html#sec-17","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"Lesson 12. How this connects to websites","x":"Why. You have learned the basics of Python. Now let's put them together and see how a website is built from these bricks. How a website works. You type an address in the browser, and the browser sends a request to a server. A server is a computer running a program, for example in Python. It looks at the address, picks the right function, and that function returns a response : the text of the page. More on the “How a website works” page. Where your lessons are here: functions — each page of the site: a function receives a request and returns a page; a dictionary — “address → function”: this is how the site chooses what to show; f-strings — put data into the page: “Hello, {name}!”; lists and loops — a feed of posts, a task list, products in a catalogue; conditions — whether the user is logged in, whether the password is correct; errors — a “404: not found” page instead of the site crashing; modules — Flask and Django, ready-made tools for websites. Flask. The second example is the same site on Flask. The line @app.route(\"/\") says: “this address opens this function”. It is really the same dictionary of addresses, only Flask creates it for you. Try it: in the first example change the a"},{"u":"start.html#next-title","p":"Python from scratch: 13 lessons in your browser","h":"What's next","x":"Now you understand the basics of Python. Next you'll learn what a website page is made of: HTML and CSS. And then you'll build your first site. HTML and CSS How a website works"},{"u":"templates.html","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","x":"Home / Templates and forms How the server builds pages A template is HTML with places for data. A form is a way to get data from the user. We look at both; examples with a “Run” button execute right in the browser."},{"u":"templates.html#jinja-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Jinja templates","x":"Flask uses Jinja, and Django's template language is very similar to it. {{ value }} outputs data and escapes HTML automatically. {% if %} and {% for %} add conditions and loops. {% extends %} and {% block %} give all pages a shared frame. Flask: a loop and a condition in a template test_client() sends a request to the app without a real server. Tests are written the same way."},{"u":"templates.html#inherit-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Template inheritance","x":"The header and footer are described once, and pages replace only their own block. templates/base.html templates/index.html In Flask the files live in the templates folder, and render_template(\"index.html\") returns the page. In Django render(request, \"index.html\") does the same."},{"u":"templates.html#escape-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Why escaping matters","x":"If you output the user's text as it is, they can put their own script on the page. This is called XSS. Escaping and the dangerous safe filter The |safe filter turns the protection off. Use it only for HTML you wrote yourself."},{"u":"templates.html#forms-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Forms in Flask","x":"Form data is in request.form . The server must check it itself: checks in the browser are easy to bypass. Flask: checking form data After a successful POST, send a redirect. Otherwise, when the page is refreshed, the browser repeats the submission and the task is added again."},{"u":"templates.html#csrf-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"CSRF protection","x":"Another site can quietly submit a form on behalf of a logged-in user. The defence is a secret token in every form. Django Protection is on by default. Add the {% csrf_token %} tag to every POST form, otherwise the server responds with error 403. Flask There is no built-in protection. The usual choice is the Flask-WTF extension: CSRFProtect(app) and a hidden token field in the form."},{"u":"templates.html#djforms-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Forms in Django","x":"A form class describes the fields, checks the data and can render itself as HTML. tasks/forms.py tasks/views.py templates/index.html"},{"u":"templates.html#sources-title","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . Jinja: template documentation Flask: templates Django 6.1: forms Django 6.1: CSRF protection"},{"u":"templates.html#sec-8","p":"Jinja templates and forms in Flask and Django","h":"Next: authentication","x":"We have forms; now let us learn to recognise the user. Open the section"},{"u":"terms.html","p":"Terms of use","h":"Terms of use","x":"Home / Terms of use Terms of use PyWeb Guide is a free learning resource. By using the site, you agree to these terms."},{"u":"terms.html#sec-1","p":"Terms of use","h":"Purpose of the site","x":"The site's materials are meant for learning the basics of web development with Python. They do not replace official documentation or professional advice."},{"u":"terms.html#sec-2","p":"Terms of use","h":"Materials and code examples","x":"The rights to the texts and design of the site belong to the author unless stated otherwise. You may freely use the code examples in your own learning and personal projects. When quoting the texts, link to the source."},{"u":"terms.html#sec-3","p":"Terms of use","h":"No warranty","x":"The materials are provided “as is”. We check the examples on the versions stated on the site but do not guarantee that they suit your task or will work in other versions. Before using the code in a production project, review it and its security yourself."},{"u":"terms.html#sec-4","p":"Terms of use","h":"External links","x":"The authors are not responsible for the content of third-party sites that links lead to."},{"u":"terms.html#sec-5","p":"Terms of use","h":"User data","x":"What data is processed when you use the site is described in the privacy policy ."},{"u":"terms.html#sec-6","p":"Terms of use","h":"Changes and feedback","x":"The terms may change: the current version is always on this page. You can ask questions in the Issues section on GitHub . In effect since 3 ноября 2026 года ."},{"u":"testing.html","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","x":"Home / Testing How to change code without breaking it A test is a small program that checks your program. In seconds it finds what you would otherwise have to click through by hand."},{"u":"testing.html#why-title","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"How a test is built","x":"Almost every test has three parts: prepare the data, perform the action, check the result. The simplest checks with assert Change the expected value in the example and run it again: you will see what a failing test looks like."},{"u":"testing.html#pytest-title","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"pytest","x":"The most popular testing library for Python. It finds test_*.py files and test_* functions by itself. Terminal test_prices.py parametrize runs one test with different data. raises checks that the code reports an error correctly. In the second test the function must reject a negative price itself."},{"u":"testing.html#flask-title","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"Flask tests","x":"The test client sends requests to the app without a server or a browser. Flask: checking a page and the API In a real project these functions live in test_app.py , and the pytest command runs them."},{"u":"testing.html#django-title","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"Django tests","x":"Django creates a separate temporary database, so your real data is not affected. tasks/tests.py Terminal"},{"u":"testing.html#what-title","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"What to test first","x":"The goal is not 100% coverage but protecting what matters. Worth checking The main scenario: create, change, delete Invalid and empty data Access: other people's data and pages without login Every bug you find: first a test, then the fix You can skip The framework's own code Simple settings without logic The exact position of elements on the page"},{"u":"testing.html#sources-title","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"Sources","x":"Links checked on 06.11.2026 . pytest: getting started Flask: testing Django 6.1: testing"},{"u":"testing.html#sec-7","p":"Testing Flask and Django: pytest and the test client","h":"Next: Git","x":"Save a working version of your code before new changes. Open the section"}];
